WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
Does DC's 'The Flash' get a gold medal?

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.650
Or does this long-awaited movie limp across
the finish line?

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:08.180
Here's what the first reviews have to say
about 'The Flash.'

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.610
"Let's get nuts."

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.290
Few films can claim to have the kind of press
as "The Flash" in the lead-up to its release.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.580
After spending years in development hell,
the movie finally started making progress

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.820
with director Andy Muschietti at the helm
and starring Ezra Miller as the Scarlet Speedster.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.720
However, Miller's well-documented instances
of criminal behavior have tainted what was

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.929
otherwise a highly anticipated movie.

00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:29.870
It's especially dire considering that the
film was meant to set up the future of the

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:34.090
DC live-action cinematic universe, which will
soon have James Gunn and Peter Safran at the

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.140
steering wheel.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.710
Warner Bros. and DC have downplayed Miller's
involvement and instead focused on how this

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:41.290
could be one of the greatest superhero movies
ever made.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.239
Everyone from Tom Cruise to Stephen King got
early screenings and raved about "The Flash"

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:46.629
to anyone who would listen.

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:49.820
Such reviews, combined with the return of
Michael Keaton as Batman, certainly makes

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.480
the film an enticing prospect, even to people
who haven't necessarily been following DC's

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.090
latest cinematic outings.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.890
But the question remains: how good is "The
Flash" really?

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.780
Does it live up to the hype, or was it all
a marketing tactic?

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.190
At first look, it seems as though "The Flash"
certainly isn't without its merits.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:08.840
It's currently standing at a 72% positive
rating on Rotten Tomatoes with 53 reviews

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.530
as of the making of this video.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:14.100
Many critics praise Ezra Miller's performance
as well as strong turns from actors new and

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.820
old to DC's universe.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.619
Ben Affleck gets time to shine as Batman before
Michael Keaton takes over those duties in

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:20.860
an alternate timeline.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.990
But cameos aside, it seems as though critics
have really taken to the emotional story beats

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.380
that make up "The Flash."

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.090
The film draws heavily from the "Flashpoint"
storyline in the comics, where Flash travels

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.619
back in time to save his mother, only to create
an alternate timeline.

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:37.510
The same happens in the movie, and the underlying
story of Barry Allen trying to find his place

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:41.530
as a superhero while maintaining his family
gives the film some much-needed weight.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:43.510
Brandon Zachary of CBR writes,

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:47.920
"The Flash is a surprisingly emotional film,
exploring concepts like the inevitability

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.640
of mortality even in a world of limitless
potential.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.020
The consistent undercurrent of grief and loss
keeps the stakes high."

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:57.509
It's these moments that apparently help the
movie stand up on its own two feet, even during

00:01:57.509 --> 00:01:58.860
more questionable moments.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.920
David Rooney of The Hollywood Reporter suggests
"The Flash" isn't perfect.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.600
Still, that core concept helps carry it through.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:05.689
According to the critic,

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:10.300
"If 'The Flash' ultimately proves uneven,
its wobbly climactic showdown far less interesting

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.629
than the more character-driven buildup, the
story's core of a young man struggling to

00:02:13.629 --> 00:02:16.340
reconcile with the loss of his mother carries
it through."

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.040
Interestingly, a film billed as the second
coming of superhero movies has more to offer

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.960
when it's focused more on characters than
spectacle, and the audience's enjoyment of

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.069
such grander moments may vary.

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:29.800
Without getting into spoilers, "The Flash"
seemingly does… a lot.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.069
There's time travel, alternate timelines,
and different versions of the same superheroes,

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:36.020
including some surprising cameos.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:39.900
The movie wants to have its cake and eat it,
too, but many critics think the film stumbles

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:40.900
more often than it flies.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.950
"There may not be a future."

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.390
In Ross Bonaime's review for Collider, there's
praise for certain story elements, but ultimately,

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:51.150
this is a film that's unable to stand on its
own two feet.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:52.170
The critic concluded,

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.709
"Unfortunately, this isn't a story that holds
together on a narrative level.

00:02:55.709 --> 00:02:59.030
Cameos and fan service are fine to have, but
the story has to be there to back them up,

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.300
and it's not quite there with 'The Flash.'"

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.760
There's little doubt certain cameos may be
enough to make general audiences forgive some

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.049
of the movie's poorer moments, but critics
are seeing right through them, especially

00:03:08.049 --> 00:03:09.480
as the film drags on.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.400
Like so many big action movies before it,
"The Flash" ends with a CGI-laden final battle

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.290
that critics are already panning.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:18.400
One negative take came from Peter Bradshaw
of The Guardian, who wrote:

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.620
"Despite some diverting touches, Miller's
smirking, gurning, mugging doppelganger performance

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.720
is a trial and in any case gets lost in the
inevitable third-act CGI battle apocalypse,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.230
which is weightlessly free of jeopardy and,
like the rest of the film, does not exactly

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.590
go by in a flash."

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.170
There's a wide range of opinions, with some
thinking the film's one of the best superhero

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:40.330
flicks of the 21st century, while others view
it as another DC letdown.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:43.470
And coming out on the heels of the nearly
universally-acclaimed "Spider-Man: Across

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.010
the Spider-Verse" may not help matters, either.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.889
People can make up their own minds when "The
Flash" comes out on June 16.

